Born in the damp, subterranean maintenance tunnels beneath Zurich during a municipal transit workers' strike, this subculture gathered around decommissioned traffic computers repurposed as generative sequencers. The scene's adherents wear heavy wool overcoats lined with vintage pocket watches and hand-tooled clockwork gears, treating urban gridlock as a canvas for high-art civil disobedience. Their rituals involve gathering at dawn in pedestrian plazas to synchronize hand-wound music boxes with the city's remaining functional streetlights, turning the morning rush hour into a massive, accidental symphony of dissent.

